msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You built a standard Quarkus application: REST endpoints, a service layer, a "
"database. Then you are given a new requirement: \"When an order is placed, "
"send an event to Kafka so the analytics team can track it.\" A week later "
"another one: \"Also notify the partner loyalty system via their REST API and "
"make sure to handle it properly when their API is down.\" And another one: "
"\"The partner is going to start sending us CSV order files.\"..."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each of these requirements is an integration task, and Quarkus already gives "
"you building blocks for all of them: Reactive Messaging for Kafka, the REST "
"client with fault tolerance for the loyalty API, a scheduled job for file "
"polling."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"But you could also hand all three to Apache Camel, which covers them with a "
"single model. Retry with backoff, error policies, JSON and CSV conversion, "
"file polling that moves handled files aside: each one is a built-in step you "
"declare on a route, a line at a time, in the same vocabulary whichever "
"system you are talking to."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"And it runs inside the application you already have. https://camel.apache."
"org/camel-quarkus/next/[Camel Quarkus] packages over 300 of Camel's https://"
"camel.apache.org/components/next/[components] as Quarkus extensions, as "
"natural to use as any other CDI bean."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In this post we start from a working Quarkus REST application and "
"progressively extend it with three integration capabilities using Camel. "
"You'll see exactly what changes in your existing code and what stays "
"untouched."
msgstr ""

msgid "The starting point"
msgstr ""

