"If you don't have the https://quarkus.io/guides/cli-tooling[Quarkus CLI] you "
"can add directly in your `pom.xml`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"the `quarkus-camel-bom` to `<dependencyManagement>` so versions are managed:"
msgstr ""

msgid "the dependencies:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you have Podman or Docker running, Quarkus Dev Services will start a "
"Kafka broker automatically. Camel Quarkus extensions are built like any "
"other Quarkus extension, so dev mode, Dev Services, configuration and native "
"image all work the way you already expect."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Camel routes are CDI beans that extend `RouteBuilder`. Quarkus discovers "
"them like any other bean:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"At this point, nothing has changed in your existing code. Your REST "
"endpoints, services, and beans are unaffected."
msgstr ""

msgid "Sending order events to Kafka"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*The first requirement:* every time an order is placed, publish an event to "
"a Kafka topic so downstream systems (analytics, warehouse, notifications) "
"can react to it."
msgstr ""

msgid "Using Camel, the change to your existing code is two lines:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`ProducerTemplate` is Camel's bridge from your existing code into a Camel "
"route. A _route_ in Camel is a processing pipeline: it starts from an input "
"(a URI like `direct:`, `kafka:`, or `file:`), applies a chain of steps "
"(transformation, filtering, logging) and then sends the result to one or "
"more outputs. Think of it as a method chain that wires I/O and processing "
"together in one place. In Camel Quarkus, `ProducerTemplate` is automatically "
"available as a CDI bean. You just need to `@Inject` it. The `sendBody` call "
"pushes the order object into the `direct:order-placed` endpoint, which is "
"the entry point of your Camel route."
msgstr ""

msgid "Here's that Camel route we need to add:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Camel route does three steps: receive the order, serialize it to JSON, "
"send it to Kafka."
msgstr ""

msgid "And the Kafka configuration to add in `application.properties`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Dev Services injects the broker address it started into `kafka.bootstrap."
"servers`, and `camel.component.kafka.brokers` passes it on to the Camel "
"Kafka component, falling back to `localhost:9092` against an external broker."
""
msgstr ""

msgid "*Try it*, create an order and watch the console:"
msgstr ""

msgid "In the logs:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Your order is persisted _and_ published to Kafka."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Extensions used:* `camel-quarkus-kafka`, `camel-quarkus-jackson`, `camel-"
"quarkus-direct`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Notifying the loyalty system"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*The second requirement:* also call the partner's loyalty API over HTTP so "
"customers earn points when they place an order. The partner's API is "
"unreliable, so you need to handle retries with backoff."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The order event Camel route already handles the Kafka step. Let's add the "
"loyalty notification: three more lines in the same route, plus an error "
"policy:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The body is still the JSON produced by `marshal().json()` for Kafka, so "
"`setHeader` tags the outgoing request as `application/json` for the partner "
"API."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `onException` block retries any failed step up to 3 times with "
"exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s). If the loyalty API returns a 500 or the "
"connection times out, Camel retries automatically. `handled(true)` means "
"that if all attempts fail, the error is logged and the route moves on. The "
"order is still safely persisted, since that happened before the Camel call. "
"Note that the policy covers every step in the route, the Kafka send "
"included, not just the HTTP call."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The route injects the partner's host from configuration with "
"`@ConfigProperty`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you want to integrate another external service later, you can add one "
"more `.to(\"http://...\")` line."
msgstr ""

msgid "*Try it*, create another order and watch both integrations fire:"
msgstr ""

msgid "The two integrations are triggered from a single `sendBody` call."
msgstr ""

msgid "*Extension used:* `camel-quarkus-http`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Ingesting files from a partner"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*The third requirement:* a partner drops CSV order files into a directory. "
"The application should parse each file, ingest the orders, and move the "
"original to a `done` directory. The failed files go to a `failed` directory."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"File ingestion has a lot of small details: polling, partial writes, moving "
"handled files aside, keeping failures separate."
msgstr ""

msgid "The whole thing is one self-contained route:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The consumer polls the directory; `readLock`, `move` and `moveFailed` on the "
"URI cover the other three. Camel's built-in `CsvDataFormat` parses the file "
"content into rows, skipping the header. After `split(body())`, each exchange "
"carries one row as a `List<String>`. `orderConverter` is a small bean that "
"maps each row to an `Order`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`orderRepository` is your existing CDI bean, referenced by name. For Camel "
"to look up the bean by name, add `@Named(\"orderRepository\")` to "
"`OrderRepository`. Since `.bean(\"name\")` resolves the method reflectively, "
"also add `@RegisterForReflection` so the lookup keeps working in native mode."
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Try it*, from the directory where you started `mvn quarkus:dev`, drop a CSV "
"file and verify:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You'll see the orders from the CSV file alongside any you created via the "
"REST API. The original file has been moved to `orders/done/`. A malformed "
"file would go to `orders/failed/` instead."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Extensions used:* `camel-quarkus-file`, `camel-quarkus-csv`, `camel-quarkus-"
"bean`"
msgstr ""

msgid "How the two fit together"
msgstr ""

msgid "In the code you already had, this is all that changed:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`@Inject ProducerTemplate producerTemplate` in `OrderResource`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`producerTemplate.sendBody(\"direct:order-placed\", order)` in "
"`OrderResource`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`@Named(\"orderRepository\")` and `@RegisterForReflection` on "
"`OrderRepository`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Everything else is new classes sitting alongside the old ones."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Beyond that, the two sides share one runtime. Any bean annotated `@Named` or "
"`@Identifier` is reachable from a route with `.bean(\"beanName\")`, or by "
"type with `.bean(MyService.class)`. Your JAX-RS endpoints keep being served "
"by Quarkus HTTP, and routes using `platform-http` can share that same server."
" Camel settings go in the same `application.properties` under the `camel.*` "
"namespace, and `quarkus dev` hot-reloads a route like any other class."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the end, the goal of this blog post was to show that Camel is easy to "
"pick up and integrates seamlessly with Quarkus. Additionally it gets more "
"valuable as the number of systems you integrate with grows. For a single "
"Kafka topic, Reactive Messaging is enough. By the third integration, having "
"the Camel route model and DSL is what makes the difference."
msgstr ""

msgid "Going further"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This post covered three patterns, but the 300+ components available as Camel "
"Quarkus extensions cover databases, cloud services, messaging systems, SaaS "
"APIs, and protocols from FTP to AS2. Each follows the same model: add the "
"`camel-quarkus-*` extension, use its URI in a route."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Camel implements the https://camel.apache.org/components/next/eips/"
"enterprise-integration-patterns.html[Enterprise Integration Patterns]. Here "
"are a few that would naturally extend this example:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Content-Based Router:* use `.choice().when()` to route priority orders to a "
"fast-track queue and standard orders to the normal flow."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Wire Tap:* use `.wireTap(\"seda:audit\")` to send a copy of the order event "
"to an audit log without slowing down the main route."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Throttle:* use `.throttle(10)` to rate-limit calls to the partner loyalty "
"API."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Dead Letter Channel:* replace the `onException` log-and-swallow with "
"`errorHandler(deadLetterChannel(\"file:orders/errors\"))` to persist failed "
"order events to disk for later inspection or reprocessing."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*More protocols:* need to call a SOAP service, send to an AMQP broker, or "
"drop files on an SFTP server? Each has a Camel Quarkus extension."
msgstr ""

msgid "To explore more:"
msgstr ""

